<article class="env-lemma" id="092V">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/092V">Lemma <span data-tag="092V">15.106.21</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring of weak dimension at most $1$. If $A \to B$ is a flat, injective, epimorphism of rings, then $A$ is integrally closed in $B$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $x \in B$ be integral over $A$. Let $A' = A[x] \subset B$. Then $A'$ is a finite ring extension of $A$ by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/02JJ" data-tag="02JJ">10.36.5</a>. To show $A = A'$ it suffices to show $A \to A'$ is an epimorphism by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/04VT" data-tag="04VT">10.107.6</a>. Note that $A'$ is flat over $A$ by assumption on $A$ and the fact that $B$ is flat over $A$ (Lemma <a href="/tag/092S" data-tag="092S">15.106.18</a>). Hence the composition 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  A' \otimes _ A A' \to B \otimes _ A A' \to B \otimes _ A B \to B  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is injective, i.e., $A' \otimes _ A A' \cong A'$ and the lemma is proved. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>